 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [BCC Marching Tetrahedra with Permutation-Based Lookup](3414-bcc-marching-tetrahedra-with-permutation-based-lookup.md)
-Extracts isosurfaces from Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) datasets by decomposing cells into tetrahedra and using permutation transformations to map a single reference tetrahedron's connectivity to all six cell types, significantly reducing GPU memory overhead for lookup tables compared to naive approaches. Choose this when working with BCC data and needing to minimize memory usage for large-scale GPU rendering while maintaining connected mesh structures.
+Extracts explicit, connected triangle mesh isosurfaces from Body-Centered Cubic (BCC) volume datasets on a GPU. It solves the problem of large lookup tables and complex branching in standard Marching Tetrahedra by using a single reference tetrahedron and applying 3D permutation transformations to handle all six cell types. Unlike the triangle-soup output of classic MT, this method produces a manifold mesh with shared vertices, enabling efficient downstream applications such as curvature rendering. Choose this when working with BCC data and needing to minimize lookup table memory while maintaining connected mesh structures.
 
 ### [Marching Tetrahedra 3 (DLC-Plane)](3632-marching-tetrahedra-3-dlc-plane.md)
-Uses a diamond crystal lattice decomposition to split cells into exactly 5 tetrahedra using only 5 vertex points, eliminating fabricated center points and the associated creases/indentations, while processing data plane-by-plane to optimize point handling. Choose this for smooth surface extraction in void areas and when avoiding the artifacts of center-point synthesis is critical.
+Generates isosurfaces from voxel data by decomposing the grid into a diamond crystal lattice structure composed of exactly 5 tetrahedra per cell, using only the 5 existing grid vertices without introducing fabricated points. It differs from standard Marching Cubes or other tetrahedral schemes by eliminating center-point synthesis, which prevents creases and indentations on data boundaries. The "Plane" variant specifically processes the grid plane-by-plane, generating a single calculation for all shared points to reduce total point count and memory overhead compared to the "Volume" variant. Choose this for smooth surface extraction when avoiding center-point artifacts is critical.
 
